## Run it in 128 GB: weights in memory, Engram on NVMe

DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash has two Engram tables (n-gram embeddings, about 101 GB each). A token reads only 24 rows of each, and the row ids depend only on the token ids. So the tables stay on NVMe and are read with `O_DIRECT` while the GPU runs; only the pruned experts and the dense path ({{ gguf.run_gib }} for `{{ gguf.run_level }}`) are loaded into memory. Upstream llama.cpp cannot do this yet: use the fork below (upstream PR #28696 plus six patches, one of which is the NVMe row store).

You need 128 GB of RAM or unified memory, an NVIDIA GPU with CUDA (measured only on a Jetson AGX Thor, sm_110; `build.sh` targets it by default, set `CUDA_ARCH` for another GPU), and about 210 GB free on a local NVMe drive for the row files (not USB, not a network share). Building the row files also needs the base model's two Engram files once (about 200 GB more, on any disk; delete them afterwards).

```bash
# 1. the fork (pinned upstream PR head + patches) and the row-file builder
git clone https://github.com/patrickbdevaney/deepseek-v4.1-flash-reap-thor && cd deepseek-v4.1-flash-reap-thor
llamacpp/setup.sh
llamacpp/build.sh                     # Jetson AGX Thor (sm_110); any other NVIDIA GPU: CUDA_ARCH=<sm, e.g. 89> llamacpp/build.sh
python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install numpy huggingface_hub

# 2. this GGUF ({{ gguf.run_level }})
.venv/bin/hf download {{ artifact.repo }} --include "*-{{ gguf.run_level }}-*.gguf" --local-dir gguf

# 3. the two Engram files of the base model, plus its config and index (see "Engram" below for sizes and sha256)
.venv/bin/hf download {{ base.repo }} config.json model.safetensors.index.json model-00047-of-00048.safetensors \
    model-00048-of-00048.safetensors --revision {{ base.revision }} --local-dir base

# 4. build and verify the row files on NVMe (a 200 GB sequential copy; the base files can be deleted afterwards)
export V41_SRC=$PWD/base V41_WORK=$PWD/work
.venv/bin/python ops/s09_engram_rowfile.py build  --dst /nvme/v41rows
.venv/bin/python ops/s09_engram_rowfile.py verify --dst /nvme/v41rows

# 5. serve (OpenAI-compatible)
LLAMA_ENGRAM_ROWFILE_DIR=/nvme/v41rows llamacpp/llama.cpp/build-thor/bin/llama-server \
    -m gguf/{{ gguf.run_file }} -ngl 999 -c 8192 --jinja --port 8080
```

`-c 8192` is the context the release gate served it at. **Context limit of this build: {{ gguf.ctx_cap }} tokens per sequence.** Past that, DeepSeek-V4.1 restricts its later layers to a set of candidate blocks that this fork does not implement yet, so the output would drift from the model without any error; the fork clamps the context there instead (patch 0006). The native CUDA runner implements the candidate blocks and is not limited this way. On a machine whose GPU memory is separate from system RAM, lower `-ngl` until the GPU part fits; the rest runs on the CPU.

